---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../site";
---

<Base
  title={"Emisar, Ryker and co:op: where each fits | " + site.name}
  description="How Emisar's remote access, co:op's local sandbox and Ryker's engineering workflow fit together, with their boundaries explained."
>
  <section class="page-intro wrap narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Tools from our team</p>
    <h1>
      Different jobs.
      <br />
      Different boundaries.
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      We build Emisar, Ryker and co:op. Each handles a different part of an
      agent's work.
    </p>
  </section>
  <div class="prose wrap narrow">
    <h2>Emisar: controlled access to infrastructure</h2>
    <p>
      An agent uses Emisar to discover and request declared actions on remote
      infrastructure. The action defines its arguments and limits. Policy
      decides whether the request can run, needs approval or is denied. A runner
      checks the action on the host, and the system records what happened.
    </p>
    <p>
      Agents can use MCP or direct CLI commands. Emisar does not run the agent's
      reasoning loop. Its runner is not a process sandbox, and an allowed
      destructive action still has real effects. The host permissions and action
      definitions remain part of the boundary.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://emisar.dev/">Explore Emisar</a> ·{" "}
      <a href="https://emisar.dev/docs/security-model">
        Read its security model
      </a>
    </p>
    <h2>co:op: a workspace for a coding agent</h2>
    <p>
      co:op runs coding agents in a sandbox around a project. It controls which
      files, credentials and network destinations the agent can reach, according
      to the selected mode and configuration. It also provides task queues and
      isolated copies of a repository for coding work.
    </p>
    <p>
      The project files an agent can write are still writable. Review changes
      and keep a recovery path. Local sandbox controls and permission to change
      remote infrastructure are separate concerns.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://coop.dryga.com/">Explore co:op</a> ·{" "}
      <a href="https://coop.dryga.com/docs.html">Read the guide</a>
    </p>
    <h2>Ryker: an AI teammate for engineering work</h2>
    <p>
      Ryker is a self-hosted AI teammate that works through Slack, GitHub and
      its web console. It can investigate an alert, answer a technical question,
      and work on requested code changes. Coding work uses co:op sessions.
      Changes to running systems use Emisar's controls.
    </p>
    <p>
      You choose the connected repositories and tools. Draft pull request
      publication needs configuration. Ryker does not merge, deploy, sign
      commits or bypass Emisar policy.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://ryker.emisar.dev/">Explore Ryker</a> ·{" "}
      <a href="https://ryker.emisar.dev/docs/">Read the documentation</a>
    </p>
    <h2>One example workflow</h2>
    <p>
      A teammate asks Ryker to investigate an alert. Ryker gathers evidence
      through its allowed tools. If a code change is needed, it can work in a
      co:op session and prepare the change for review. If the team requests an
      infrastructure action, Emisar applies the access rules and any required
      approval before execution.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each part needs its own configuration and checks. A successful request to
      one component does not prove that the whole workflow is safe or correctly
      configured.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
